[Puzzle "The b-pawn push pays off"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "7.Bxf7+"]
[End "7.Bxf7+"]
[Hint "The knight on a5 attacks your bishop. Make the bishop count before it is taken."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 h6?! 5. b4 Bb6 6. b5 Na5 7. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gc4f7] The bishop takes f7 with check before the knight can take it.}
(7. Nxe5? {Wins a pawn, but after 7...Nxc4 8.Nxc4 d5 Black has the bishop pair and an equal game.} 7... Nxc4 8. Nxc4 d5 {Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
(7. Be2?! {Saves the bishop, but the moment is gone: Black develops and castles in peace.} 7... Nf6 8. d3 O-O 9. O-O {Black's pieces come out easily, and White's chance has gone. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
7... Kf8 {Black's best. 7...Kxf7 is worse: 8.Nxe5+ takes the pawn with check, and Black's king is left exposed in the middle of the board, a poor position for Black. Stockfish: +5.4 after 7...Kxf7, winning for White, against +4.4 after 7...Kf8. After the puzzle you can play 7...Kxf7 through under Other lines.}
(7... Kxf7 {Takes the bishop, but the king comes out into the open.} 8. Nxe5+ {Takes the e5 pawn with check, and the knight eyes g6.}
8... Kf8 {Back to f8, but this walks into a fork.}
(8... Ke6 {Black's best try, but the king is stuck in the middle of the board.} 9. Qh5 Qf6 10. d4 g6 11. Qg4+ {Stockfish: +4.3, winning for White.})
(8... Ke8 {Runs into mate.} 9. Qh5+ Ke7 10. Ba3+ d6 11. Qf7#)
9. Ng6+ {[%cal Gg6h8,Gg6f8] A fork: check, and the knight attacks the rook on h8. No pawn guards g6: the f-pawn is gone and the h-pawn is on h6. 8...Ke7 runs into the same fork.}
9... Ke8 10. Nxh8 {White wins the rook and has a rook and two pawns for the bishop. Stockfish: +5.9, winning for White.})
8. Nxe5 {[%cal Ge5f7] Wins the e5 pawn and guards the bishop on f7. Now the knight eyes g6, next to Black's king.}
8... Qf6 {[%cal Rf6f2,Rb6f2] Hits the knight and lines up with the bishop on b6 against f2.}
9. d4! {[%csl Gd4] Stops ...Qxf2, which would be mate. The pawn blocks the bishop on b6 and also guards the knight on e5.}
(9. Ng6+! {Also good: the fork one move early.} Kxf7 10. Nxh8+ {White wins the rook here too, but still has to meet the threat of ...Qxf2 mate, with d4, Qh5 or castling. Stockfish: +2.3, winning for White.})
9... d6 {Attacks the knight. If it moves away, the bishop on f7 loses its guard.}
10. Ng6+! {[%cal Gg6h8,Gg6f8] A fork: check, and the knight attacks the rook on h8.}
(10. Bxg8? {Takes a knight, but Black takes one back and White's attack is over. The fork wins far more.} 10... dxe5 11. Bb3 Nxb3 12. axb3 exd4 {White is only a pawn up, and Black has the two bishops. Stockfish: +1.4, clearly better for White, but far less than after 10.Ng6+.})
10... Kxf7
11. Nxh8+ {[%cal Gg6h8] White wins the rook. The knight on h8 is trapped and will be lost, but Black's king has no shelter.}
11... Kf8 12. O-O g6 13. Qd3 Qxh8 {Black wins the knight back. White has a rook and two pawns for two minor pieces. Stockfish: +3.2, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Same sacrifice, no fork"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Defend the king"]
[Solve "7...Kxf7"]
[End "8...Kf8"]
[Hint "In puzzle 1 Black's h-pawn was on h6. Here it is still on h7. What does that change?"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 a6?! {Gives the bishop on c5 a retreat on a7 and covers b5, so b4-b5 can be met by ...axb5. But it develops nothing. Stockfish: +1.2 after 5.d4, against +0.1 after 4...Nf6.}
5. b4 {Gains space, but with ...a6 played the bishop can drop back to a7. Stockfish: +0.2, so 5.d4 was stronger.}
(5. d4! {White's best: the center pawns advance while Black is a move behind.} 5... exd4 6. cxd4 Ba7 7. O-O d6 8. h3 {White has a big center and easy development. Stockfish: +1.4, clearly better for White.})
5... Bb6 {Fine, though 5...Ba7 is a touch better.}
6. b5? {Chases the knight, but the pawn on a6 can take on b5.}
6... Na5 {Saves the knight and hits the bishop on c4.}
(6... axb5! {The point of ...a6.} 7. Bxb5 Nf6 8. d3 O-O 9. O-O {Black has the open a-file and is ahead in development. Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.})
7. Bxf7+? {The trick from puzzle 1. Here it doesn't work.}
(7. Nxe5! {White's best, though the game stays balanced. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
7... Kxf7! {Take it. With the h-pawn still on h7, the square g6 is covered, so there is no knight fork against your king and rook.}
(7... Kf8? {Declining lets White keep the extra pawn, and your king has already lost the right to castle.} 8. Bb3 axb5 9. d4 Nxb3 10. Qxb3 {White has won back the pawn, keeps the f7 pawn, and Black's king is stuck on f8. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.})
8. Nxe5+ {Takes the pawn with check, as in puzzle 1.}
8... Kf8! {[%hint Find the square where the king is safe from checks and from Ng6+.] The safest square. The queen has no check, and Ng6+ is impossible because the pawn on h7 guards g6. White has only two pawns for the bishop.}
(8... Ke8? {Back to e8 lets the queen in.} 9. Qh5+ g6 10. Nxg6 Nf6 11. Qe5+ Kf7 12. Nxh8+ {White gets play against your king and takes the rook. Stockfish: −0.5, only slightly better for Black, far less than after 8...Kf8.})
(8... Ke7!? {Also good. Stockfish rates it close to 8...Kf8, though the king is a little more exposed on e7.} 9. d4 d6 10. Nd3 Nf6 11. O-O {Stockfish: −3.1, winning for Black.})
9. d4 d6 {Kicks the knight away from e5.}
10. Nf3 axb5 {Black also takes the b5 pawn and is a piece for a pawn up. Stockfish: −3.5, winning for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "Escape the Møller trap"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Escape the trap"]
[Solve "9...d5"]
[Hint "Your bishop on b4 is attacked. Counterattack instead of grabbing on c3."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 Nxe4 8. O-O Nxc3?! 9. bxc3 d5! {[%cal Gd5c4] Hits the bishop on c4 and leaves your own bishop where it is.}
(9... Bxc3? {Grabs a second pawn, but 10.Ba3! stops you from castling, and White's attack wins.} 10. Ba3! {[%cal Ga3f8] The bishop covers f8, so Black can't castle kingside. White doesn't mind losing the rook on a1.}
10... Bxa1 {Takes the rook at once. Black is a rook and two pawns up.}
(10... d5 {Black's best try: it hits the bishop on c4 before taking the rook.} 11. Bb5 {Pins the knight on c6 against Black's king.} 11... Bxa1 {Black takes the rook and is now a rook and two pawns up.} 12. Re1+ Be6 13. Qc2 {White threatens Ne5, hitting the pinned knight a third time, and Black's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: +3.1, winning for White, even a rook down.})
(10... Bb4 11. Bxb4 Nxb4 12. Re1+ Kf8 13. Qb3 {Attacks the knight on b4 and lines up behind the bishop against f7. Stockfish: +5.1, winning for White.})
(10... d6 11. Rc1 Ba5 12. Qa4 Bd7 13. d5 {Attacks the knight on c6. It is the only guard of the bishop on a5, so White wins a piece. Stockfish: +4.6, winning for White.})
11. Re1+ {Check on the open e-file. Black can only block, and every block loses material.} 11... Ne7 12. Bxe7 {Takes the blocking knight. If Black doesn't take back with the queen, Bxd8 wins it.} 12... Qxe7 13. Rxe7+ {The rook takes the queen with check.} 13... Kxe7 14. Qe1+ {Another check, and the queen heads for a1.} 14... Kf8 15. Qxa1 {White wins back the bishop. Material is level again, but Black's rooks and bishop are still at home and the king is stuck on f8, while White's queen, bishop and knight are all active. Stockfish: +7.3, winning for White.})
10. cxb4 dxc4 {Takes back the piece. Black is a pawn up, but White's lead in development makes up for it. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.}
*

[Puzzle "Refute the fork trick"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "6...Nxc4"]
[End "6...Nxc4"]
[Hint "The pawn on d4 forks your bishop and knight. Can one of them capture something first?"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nxe5? Nxe5 6. d4 Nxc4! {[%cal Ge5c4] Take the bishop first. Now only your bishop on c5 is attacked, and after White takes it you are still a piece for a pawn up.}
(6... Bd6? {Saves the bishop, but the fork trick works: after 7.dxe5 Bxe5 White has won the piece back, and the game is equal.} 7. dxe5 Bxe5 8. O-O O-O 9. Bd3 {Material is level and the position is quiet. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
(6... Nxe4?! {Equalizes, but misses the win. Take on c4 first.} 7. Nxe4 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Nxc4 9. Bxb4 d5 {Material is level. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
7. dxc5 d5 {Guards the knight on c4 and frees the bishop on c8. Black is a knight for a pawn up, and White's pawn on e4 is attacked twice. Stockfish: −3.9, winning for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "Refute the h4 gambit"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Escape the trap"]
[Solve "9...d5"]
[End "9...d5"]
[Hint "Before grabbing on f2, deal with White's bishop and pawn aiming at f7."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. Bg5 h6 7. h4?! {A famous gambit: White offers the bishop to open the h-file for the rook. Stockfish: −4.2 after 7...hxg5!, so Black should take.}
7... hxg5! {Takes the bishop.}
8. hxg5 {Opens the h-file and attacks the knight on f6.}
8... Ng4! {Steps away with an eye on f2. Whatever White does now, Stockfish rates Black clearly winning (about −4.5).}
9. g6 {Sets the trap: if Black grabs on f2, 10.Nxe5! wins.}
9... d5! {[%cal Gd5c4] Blocks the bishop's line to f7 (c4, d5, e6, f7) and attacks the bishop at the same time. Only now is ...Nxf2 safe.}
(9... Nxf2? {Grabs the pawn and forks queen and rook, but it walks into the trap: see the next puzzle.} 10. Nxe5! Nxh1 11. gxf7+ Rxf7 12. Bxf7+ Kf8 13. Qh5 {Stockfish: +5.4, winning for White.})
(9... d6!? {Also keeps the win: it guards e5, so the Nxe5 trick no longer works. 9...d5 is stronger because it also hits the bishop.} 10. Bxf7+ Rxf7 11. gxf7+ Kxf7 12. Rf1 {Stockfish: −1.6, clearly better for Black.})
(9... Bxf2+!? {Also wins for Black, though 9...d5 is cleaner.} 10. Ke2 Nd4+ 11. Nxd4 exd4 12. gxf7+ Rxf7 13. Bxf7+ {Stockfish: −4.5, winning for Black.})
10. Nxd5 Nxf2 11. Nxe5 Nxd1 12. gxf7+ Rxf7 13. Rh8+ Kxh8 14. Nxf7+ Kh7 15. Nxd8 Nxd8 {The checks run out, and Black has an extra piece for two pawns. Stockfish: −4.5, winning for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "The h4 trap springs"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "10.Nxe5"]
[End "13.Qh5"]
[Hint "Ignore the fork. Which move adds a third attacker on f7 and opens a line for your queen?"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. Bg5 h6 7. h4?! hxg5 8. hxg5 Ng4 9. g6 Nxf2? {Forks the queen and the rook on h1, but leaves Black's king short of defenders. 9...d5! was the right move (previous puzzle).}
10. Nxe5! {[%cal Ge5f7,Gc4f7,Gg6f7] Leaves queen and rook hanging. The knight is the third attacker on f7, and the queen now has the d1-h5 diagonal.}
(10. Bxf7+? {Too early: the attack fizzles out.} 10... Rxf7 11. Nxe5 Rf4 12. Qh5 Nxh1 13. Qh7+ Kf8 {Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
(10. gxf7+? {Also too early.} 10... Rxf7 11. Bxf7+ Kxf7 12. Nxe5+ Nxe5 13. Qh5+ g6 {Stockfish: −3.4, winning for Black.})
10... Nxh1 {Takes the rook. This is Black's best try.}
(10... Nxd1?? {Takes the queen and gets mated.} 11. gxf7+ Rxf7 12. Bxf7+ Kf8 13. Rh8+ Ke7 14. Nd5+ Kd6 15. Nc4# {Mate.})
(10... Qh4?? {Defends along the h-file, but loses the queen and then the king.} 11. Rxh4 Nxd3+ 12. cxd3 Be3 13. gxf7+ Rxf7 14. Qh5 Bf2+ 15. Kf1 Bxh4+ 16. Qxf7+ Kh8 17. Qh5# {Mate.})
11. gxf7+! {[%hint Start with a check.] Check, and the pawn takes on f7.}
(11. Qh5?! {Still good, but Black trades queens with check and keeps more.} 11... Qh4+ 12. Qxh4 Bf2+ 13. Qxf2 Nxf2 {Stockfish: +3.2, winning for White.})
11... Rxf7 12. Bxf7+! {[%hint Another capture with check.] Takes the rook with check.}
12... Kf8 13. Qh5! {[%hint Your queen has a new diagonal. Use it.] The queen comes in and threatens mate: Qh8+ Ke7, Nd5+ Kd6, Nc4#.}
13... Qh4+ {Black has to trade queens.} 14. Qxh4 Bf2+ 15. Qxf2 Nxf2 16. Kxf2 Nxe5 17. Bb3 {The dust settles with White a pawn up and better placed. Stockfish: +4.8, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Escape the Blackburne trap"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Escape the trap"]
[Solve "5.O-O"]
[End "5.O-O"]
[Hint "Your knight on e5 and the pawn on g2 are both attacked. Don't grab more material."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nd4 4. Nxe5? Qg5 5. O-O! {Castling protects g2 and brings the rook toward the center. Black wins the knight on e5, but White gains time against Black's queen and wins the piece back later.}
(5. Bxf7+!? {Playable, but castling is better. Here White gets only two pawns for the knight, while after 5.O-O White wins the piece back. Stockfish: −0.8, against −0.5 for 5.O-O; slightly better for Black either way.} 5... Kd8 6. O-O Qxe5 7. c3 Ne6 8. d3 {White has two pawns for the knight, and Black's king can no longer castle. But Black still has the extra piece. Stockfish: −0.6, slightly better for Black.})
(5. Nxf7? {Forks queen and rook, but walks into the trap.} 5... Qxg2 {Ignores the fork and hits the rook on h1.} 6. Rf1 {Saves the rook, but now the king has no room.} (6. Ng5 {Avoids the mate, but after 6...Qxh1+ 7.Bf1 Black has won the rook.} 6... Qxh1+ 7. Bf1 {Stockfish: −8.7, winning for Black.}) 6... Qxe4+ {Check, and the bishop has to block.} 7. Be2 Nf3# {White's own pieces take every square the king could use.})
5... Qxe5 {Black wins the knight.}
6. c3 {Kicks the knight away from d4.}
6... Ne6 7. d4 {Gains more time on the queen.}
7... Qxe4 {Takes a second pawn, but the queen is now on the open e-file.}
8. Re1 Qh4 9. g3 {Kicks the queen again.}
9... Qd8 10. d5 {[%cal Re1e8] The knight on e6 is pinned against Black's king, and the pawn attacks it. White wins the piece back. Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.}
*

[Puzzle "Punish ...exd4 with e5"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "7.e5"]
[End "8.exf6"]
[Hint "Black's knight on f6 is the only piece guarding Black's king. Can you chase it with tempo?"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nd4 4. O-O Nxf3+ 5. Qxf3 Nf6?! {Develops, but lets White open the center with 6.d4 (Stockfish: +1.7).}
6. d4 exd4? {Grabs a pawn, but opens the e-file in front of Black's king. 6...d5! was the move (+1.7).}
7. e5! {[%cal Ge5f6] Hits the knight while Black's d-pawn and king are still at home.}
(7. Qb3?! {Hits b7 and f7, but lets Black off the hook.} 7... d5 8. exd5 Qd6 {Stockfish: +1.3, clearly better for White.})
7... d5 {Counterattacks the bishop on c4.}
8. exf6 {[%hint Take what is offered.] Takes the knight. Black wins the bishop back, but the pawn on f6 stays deep in Black's camp.}
(8. Bg5? {Pins the knight, but lets Black keep the pieces.} 8... h6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 {Stockfish: +1.1, clearly better for White.})
8... dxc4
9. Bg5! {[%cal Gg5f6] Guards the pawn on f6 with a second piece. Next fxg7 would hit the rook on h8 and the bishop on f8, and Re1+ is coming too.}
(9. Re1+?! {Still wins, but gives Black time to block.} 9... Be6 10. Bg5 Qd7 11. Qxb7 Rc8 {Stockfish: +3.8, winning for White, against +5.2 after 9.Bg5.})
9... Qd6 10. Re1+ Be6 11. Qxb7 {Material is level, but Black's king is stuck in the middle and the pawn on f6 cramps Black's position. Stockfish: +5.2, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Rousseau: where does the knight go?"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Save the piece"]
[Solve "5.Nd4"]
[End "5.Nd4"]
[Hint "Your knight on f3 is attacked. Find a square that is safe from Black's queen and pawns."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 f5?! {The Rousseau Gambit: Black attacks e4.}
4. exf5 {Takes the pawn. Declining with 4.d3 is simpler, as in the White course.}
4... e4 {Hits the knight on f3.}
5. Nd4! {[%cal Gd4c6,Gd4f5] The knight goes to the center, attacks the knight on c6 and guards the pawn on f5.}
(5. Ng1!? {Playable: the retreat is safe, but it costs time. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.})
(5. Ng5?? {[%cal Rd8g5] Black's queen has an open line to g5.} 5... Qxg5 6. O-O Qxf5 7. Nc3 {Black has won the knight. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.})
(5. Nh4?? {[%cal Rd8h4] The same diagonal reaches h4.} 5... Qxh4 6. d3 Nf6 7. Nc3 {Black has won the knight. Stockfish: −6.1, winning for Black.})
(5. Nc3? {Lets the knight be taken for a pawn.} 5... exf3 6. Qxf3 Qh4 7. Bxg8 Rxg8 {Stockfish: −1.4, clearly better for Black.})
5... Nf6 {Black's best: develops and leaves the knight on c6 where it is.}
(5... Nxd4? {Takes the knight, but opens the queen's way to h5: see the next puzzle.} 6. Qh5+ g6 7. fxg6 {Stockfish: +3.2, winning for White.})
6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. d4 d5 8. Be2 {The position is balanced: Black has pawns in the center for the gambit pawn. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.}
*

[Puzzle "Promote with a discovered check"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Tactics"]
[Solve "8.g7+"]
[End "9.gxh8=Q"]
[Hint "Your queen on h5 is attacked, but your g-pawn stands between the queen and Black's king."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 f5?! 4. exf5 e4 5. Nd4 Nxd4? {Opens the d1-h5 diagonal for White's queen. 5...Nf6 was best.}
6. Qh5+ {Check on the diagonal that Black's f-pawn used to guard.} g6
7. fxg6 {Threatens to win the rook on h8 with g7+.} Nf6? {Attacks the queen, but walks into a discovered check.}
(7... Qf6 {Black's best, but 8.g7+ still wins the rook.} 8. g7+ Kd8 9. gxh8=Q Qxh8 {Stockfish: +2.2, winning for White.})
8. g7+! {[%cal Gh5e8] The pawn moves and uncovers check from the queen on h5. It also attacks the rook on h8.}
(8. Bf7+? {A check, but it wins far less.} 8... Ke7 9. Qc5+ d6 10. Qxd4 hxg6 {Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
(8. Qe5+? {Saves the queen with check, but the attack is gone.} 8... Ne6 9. Bxe6 dxe6 {Stockfish: −5.2, winning for Black.})
8... Nxh5 {Black takes the queen, since nothing else answers the check.}
9. gxh8=Q {[%hint Take the rook and choose your new piece carefully.] Takes the rook and makes a new queen. White wins back the queen with a rook to spare.}
9... Qf6 10. Qxh7 Nf4 11. Qxe4+ {White is well ahead in material. Stockfish: +4.6, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Légal's knight jump"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "6.Nxe5"]
[End "8.Qb5+"]
[Hint "Your knight on f3 is pinned to the queen, not to the king. Can it move anyway?"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. Nc3 Bg4 5. h3 Bh5? {Keeps the pin, but the knight is pinned only to the queen.}
6. Nxe5! {[%cal Gf3e5] The pinned knight moves anyway and takes a pawn. If Black takes the queen, it's mate.}
(6. g4!? {Playable: it breaks the pin, but slowly, and Black's bishop just steps back.} 6... Bg6 7. d4 Nf6 8. d5 Nb8 {Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White, against +1.9 after 6.Nxe5.})
6... Nxe5 {Black's best: it takes the knight and attacks the bishop on c4.}
(6... Bxd1?? {Takes the queen and walks into Légal's mate.} 7. Bxf7+ {Check. The king's only square is e7.} 7... Ke7 8. Nd5# {Mate with three minor pieces. The knight on d5 gives check, the knight on e5 covers d7, and the bishop covers e6 and e8.})
(6... dxe5 {Takes back with the pawn, but the bishop on h5 still hangs.} 7. Qxh5 g6 8. Qf3 Qf6 9. Qxf6 Nxf6 10. d3 {White is a pawn up and has the two bishops. Stockfish: +3.1, winning for White.})
(6... Qg5 {Counterattacks g2, but White's knight drops back with tempo.} 7. Ng4 f5 8. d3 Qg6 9. exf5 Qxf5 10. Ne3 {White is a pawn up, and Black's king is still in the center. Stockfish: +3.4, winning for White.})
7. Qxh5 {[%hint The knight has left f3. What can your queen take now?] Takes the bishop. White is a pawn up for now, and the knight on e5 attacks the bishop on c4.}
(7. Bb5+? {A check, but it gives Black time to keep the extra piece.} 7... c6 8. Qxh5 cxb5 9. Nxb5 {White has two pawns for the bishop. Stockfish: −3.5, winning for Black.})
7... Nxc4 {Takes the bishop, so Black is a piece up for a moment.}
(7... Nf6 {Hits the queen first.} 8. Qe2 Nxc4 9. Qxc4 c6 10. Qb3 {White is a pawn up all the same. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White.})
8. Qb5+! {[%hint Find a check that also attacks the knight on c4.] [%cal Gb5c4] A fork: check, and the queen attacks the knight on c4.}
(8. Qd5? {Attacks the knight too, but without check Black saves it.} 8... Nb6 9. Qxb7 Qc8 10. Qxc8+ Rxc8 {Black keeps the extra piece. Stockfish: −3.2, winning for Black.})
8... c6 9. Qxc4 Be7 10. Qb3 {White is a clean pawn up and better developed. Stockfish: +2.3, winning for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Ng5 punishes 4...Nf6"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Punish the mistake"]
[Solve "5.Ng5"]
[End "7.Bb5+"]
[Hint "Count the attackers and defenders of f7."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. Nc3 Nf6? {Natural, but with ...d6 already played, the bishop on f8 is shut in and f7 is short of defenders. Black's sensible fourth moves, such as 4...Be7, 4...Be6 or 4...Bg4, keep White's edge to about +0.3 to +0.6.}
5. Ng5! {[%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] Two attackers on f7, and only the king defends it. Black has to block the bishop with ...d5, and that costs a pawn.}
(5. O-O?! {Safe, but it lets Black off the hook.} 5... Be7 6. d4 Bg4 7. dxe5 dxe5 {Black has caught up in development. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
(5. d4?! {Natural, but it misses the chance.} 5... Bg4 6. Bb5 Nd7 7. d5 Nd4 {Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
5... d5 {The only decent defense: it blocks the bishop's line to f7.}
(5... Be6 {Covers f7 with the bishop, but the bishop is taken.} 6. Nxe6 fxe6 7. Bxe6 {White has won a pawn, and Black's king has lost its shelter. Stockfish: +2.6, winning for White.})
(5... Qe7 {Defends f7 with the queen, but it isn't enough.} 6. Bxf7+ Kd7 7. Be6+ Ke8 8. Bxc8 Rxc8 {White has won a pawn and the bishop pair, and Black can no longer castle. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.})
6. exd5 {[%hint The pawn on d5 attacks your bishop. Which capture keeps the pressure on f7?] Takes the pawn and keeps the bishop aimed at f7.}
(6. Nxd5? {Trades off the attacker on d5, but Black's pieces come alive.} 6... Nxd5 7. d3 Bb4+ 8. c3 {Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.})
6... Na5 {Attacks the bishop. This is Black's best try to win the pawn back.}
(6... Nd4 {Also possible, but White simply castles and stays a pawn up.} 7. O-O Bg4 8. f3 Bh5 {Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
(6... Nxd5? {Takes the pawn back, but it walks into the Fried Liver: see the next puzzle.} 7. Nxf7! Kxf7 8. Bxd5+ {Stockfish: +5.6, winning for White.})
7. Bb5+! {[%hint The bishop is attacked. Can you move it with gain of time?] Check first. Black has to block, and White keeps the extra pawn.}
(7. d3!? {Playable: it saves the bishop, but without check Black gets time to hit back.} 7... h6 8. Nge4 Nxe4 9. Nxe4 Nxc4 10. dxc4 {White has given up the bishop pair. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White, against +1.4 after 7.Bb5+.})
7... c6 8. dxc6 bxc6 9. Bd3 {White is a pawn up with a solid position. Stockfish: +1.5, clearly better for White.}
*

[Puzzle "Fried Liver in the Paris"]
[Type "puzzle"]
[Theme "Tactics"]
[Solve "7.Nxf7"]
[End "7.Nxf7"]
[Hint "Your knight on g5 is attacked. Before you save it, look at f7: only Black's king defends it."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. Nc3 Nf6? 5. Ng5 d5 6. exd5 Nxd5? 7. Nxf7! {[%cal Gg5f7] The knight was attacked by Black's queen anyway. Now it takes on f7 and drags the king out.}
(7. Qf3? {Lines up on f7, but the knight on g5 is hanging.} 7... Qxg5 8. Bxd5 Qf6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Qxf6 gxf6 {Material is level, and Black's broken pawns are White's only gain. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
(7. Nxd5? {Takes the knight on d5, but leaves the one on g5 hanging.} 7... Qxg5 8. Nxc7+ Kd8 9. d3 Qg6 {White has won a pawn, and the knight on c7 attacks the rook on a8, but the knight is far from home and Black's queen is active. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.})
7... Kxf7 8. Bxd5+ {Takes the knight back with check. White has won back the piece and is a pawn up. Black's king can no longer castle and stands in the open, and Bxc6+ followed by Qh5+ threatens the e5 pawn as well. Stockfish: +5.8, winning for White.}
(8. Nxd5?! {Wins back the piece too, but without check, so Black gets time to bring out a piece.} 8... Be6 9. Qf3+ Ke8 10. O-O {Stockfish: +3.6, winning for White, against +5.8 after 8.Bxd5+.})
8... Ke8 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Qh5+ Kd7 11. Qf5+ Ke8 12. Qxe5+ {White wins a second pawn, and Black's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: +5.4, winning for White.}
*
